 36   c  TITL$Nuclear Structure and Decay Data for A=36 Isobars                                    
 36   c  AUTH$Jun Chen and Lijie Sun                                            
 36   c  INST$Facility for Rare Isotope Beams, Michigan State University, East  
 36  2c  Lansing, Michigan 48824, USA.                                          
 36   c  ABST$This work presents a comprehensive and critical evaluation of     
 36  2c  experimental nuclear spectroscopic data from reactions and decays for  
 36  3c  all 11 known nuclides with mass number 36 (Ne, Na, Mg, Al, Si, P, S,   
 36  4c  Cl, Ar, K, Ca). Recommended values are produced for level energies,
 36  5c  spins and parities, half-lives, and radiation properties including     
 36  6c  energies, branching ratios, and multipolarities of |g rays, as well as 
 36  7c  characteristics of |b and |a radiation decays, based on a rigorous     
 36  8c  assessment of all available experimental data. Discrepancies among     
 36  9c  existing results are carefully addressed. Attempts to search for       
 36  Ac  {+36}Ne and {+36}Na have been made but they still remain unobserved.   
 36  Bc  Data on excited states for {+36}Al are scarce (only two with unknown   
 36  Cc  energies), while those for {+36}Mg, {+36}Si, {+36}P, {+36}K and {+36}Ca
 36  Dc  are limited. {+36}Ar is the most extensively studied nuclide in this   
 36  Ec  mass chain via various decays and reactions, followed by {+36}Cl and   
 36  Fc  {+36}S. This work supersedes the earlier evaluation of A=36 nuclides by
 36  Gc  2012Ni01. The recommended datasets presented in this work provide a    
 36  Hc  consistent reference for nuclear structure and decay studies, as well  
 36  Ic  as related applications.                                               
 36   c  CUT$Literature available up to September 30, 2026 has been consulted,  
 36  2c  with the help of the primary bibliographic source, the NSR database    
 36  3c  (2014Pr09) available at Brookhaven National Laboratory web page: 36 #c 
 36  4c  www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/                                                  
 36   c  COMM$The statistical analysis of |g-ray data and deduced level schemes 
 36  2c  is carried out through a suite of current versions of computer codes   
 36  3c  available at the IAEA-NDS web page of ENSDF codes                      
 36  4c  (https://www-nds.iaea.org/public/ensdf_pgm) such as GLSC, Java-RULER,  
 36  5c  BrIccMixing, and ConsistencyCheck. The direct feedings to excited      
 36  6c  states in |b{+-} and |e decays have generally been computed from       
 36  7c  I(|g+ce) intensity balances at each level; the associated log {Ift}    
 36  8c  values are calculated using the BetaShape code. Theoretical total      
 36  9c  conversion coefficients are calculated using the version v2.3e of BrIcc
 36  Ac  code (2008Ki07) for frozen-orbit option with an implicit uncertainty of
 36  Bc  1.4% when not stated. Q values and particle separation energies have   
 36  Cc  been adopted from 2021Wa16 (AME2020). Static magnetic dipole moments   
 36  Dc  are taken from 2025StZZ (long-lived states) and 2020StZV (short-lived  
 36  Ec  states) evaluations, and static electric quadrupole moments from       
 36  Fc  2021StZZ evaluation, when available in these publications. Nuclear rms 
 36  Gc  charge radii have been adopted from 2013An02 evaluation. In cases where
 36  Hc  weighted averaging procedures have been used, the assigned uncertainty 
 36  Ic  in the result is generally not lower than the lowest uncertainty in the
 36  Jc  data points used in the procedure.                                     
 36   c  ACKN$Earlier evaluation (2012Ni01), and compilations available in the  
 36  2c  XUNDL database provided valuable resources for the present work. The   
 36  3c  evaluators are grateful to the reviewers at different stages of this   
 36  4c  work, and to the personnel at the National Nuclear Data Center (NNDC)  
 36  5c  at Brookhaven National Laboratory for facilitating this work.          
 36   c  PERM$Contact: chenj@frib.msu.edu                                       
 36   c  FUND$Work supported by the Office of Nuclear Physics, Office of        
 36  2c  Science, U.S. Department of Energy, through DE-SC0016948 grant at      
 36  3c  Michigan State University                                              
                                                                                
